   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 730 of the laws of 2019, amending the executive law\nrelating to the authority of the New York black car operators' injury\ncompensation fund, inc., in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8568",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8568-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 22,
       "fullName": "Andrew Gounardes",
       "imgName": "1049_Andrew_Gounardes.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1229,
       "sessionMemberId": 1809,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "GOUNARDES"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2, Chap 730 of 2019",
    "lawSection": "Executive",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8568\n\nSPONSOR: SAVINO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 730 of the laws of 2019, amending the executive\nlaw relating to the authority of the New York black car operators' inju-\nry compensation fund, inc., in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nProvides a three year extender to certain provisions and benefits of the\nblack car fund, specifically vision and telemedicine benefits\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nAmends Section 2 of chapter 730 of the laws of 2019 as amended by Chap-\nter 64 of the laws of 2020 to extend these provisions for an additional\nthree years\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nIn 1999 New York State, with the support of the International Associ-\nation of Machinists, the AFL-CIO, and the owners of black car companies,\ncreated the Black Car Fund to provide workers compensation insurance for\nblack car operators paid for with a surcharge on every black car ride.\nThe fund now covers over 140,000 black car operators around the state\nincluding all Uber and Lyft drivers with a surcharge of 2.5%.\n\nIn July of 2018, in an effort to control workers' compensation costs\nwhile increasing the safety and wellbeing of black car operators, their\npassengers, and the public, the Black Car Fund launched two new cost\ncontainment strategies. The first was to offer drivers in New York State\nfree vision benefits including a free annual eye exam and access to a\nfree pair of eyeglasses-when necessary. The second was to offer drivers\naccess to free telemedicine services as. a low-cost alternative to emer-\ngency room visits and costly in-person follow-up, when appropriate. As\npart of the purchased telemedicine plan drivers may also call a doctor\noutside of a work-related injury.\n\nIn May of 2019, the New York State Workers' Compensation Board raised\nconcerns that these two new cost containment programs may not meet a\nstrict reading of the current law, which authorizes The Black Car Fund\nto spend money for \"securing the payment of workers' compensation\" and\n\"any statutory penalty resulting from the failure to secure such\npayment.\"(WCL section 11). This legislation would make it clear that The\nBlack Car Fund can spend an amount of money equal to a surcharge of 0.5%\nfor benefits that do not fall within the strict reading of the law.\n\nAdditionally, Article 6-F, Section I60-ii of the executive law, as added\nby chapter 49 of the laws of 1999, to add a new paragraph (5) that clar-\nifies that the Black Car Fund has the authority to provide additional\nbenefits, consistent with its plan of operation. The law sets the amount\nof money the Black Car Fund can spend on benefits other than workers'\ncompensation payments and penalties related thereto at an amount equal\nto a surcharge of 0.5%.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe additional benefits provides to drivers at no cost from the Black\nCar Fund have been beneficial and have filled in for gaps in traditional\nhealth insurance, therefore the authorization for the fund to provide\nthese benefits should be extended.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022 Session - New Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
